yield: whatever cut and weight you've got — see notes for matching the variant to it
ingredients:
  - item: brisket
    note: point, flat, or a thin cheap flat — the cut on hand decides which variant fits, see notes
components:
  - name: the drum (equipment notes)
    notes: |
      Oil drum, wood burning on a lower grate, meat on the upper grate, an upturned steel
      mixing bowl standing in for a lid. No damper, no vents — there's no way to throttle it
      between hot and not hot once it's lit, it just runs at whatever the fire happens to be
      doing. The only real lever you have is how long you let the wood burn down before the
      meat goes on: fresh flame is hot, sooty and scorches sugar; a bed of glowing coals an
      hour or so in is hotter-tasting, cleaner, and far more forgiving. Build the fire well
      before you need it and let it settle to coals, not flame, before anything touches the
      grate.

      That makes it a searing tool, not a smoker. Real barbecue smoke is low, thin and blue,
      held at one stable temperature for hours, and that needs a chamber you can hold a
      temperature in. This rig can't do that for fifteen minutes let alone all afternoon, so
      every low-and-slow phase below happens in the oven, full stop. The drum's job is short,
      hot, watched passes measured in minutes: kickstarting bark and picking up whatever
      smoke sticks at the start, or firming bark and glazing at the end. Neither needs temp
      control, just proximity, timing and attention.

      No lid also means smoke that would normally circulate and cling just rises past the
      meat once and is gone. Expect a fraction of what a closed smoker deposits in the same
      window — real wood exposure here is a flavour bonus layered on top of liquid smoke,
      never a substitute for it.

      If fat dripping onto the fire flares up, that's normal — it just means move the meat to
      whichever part of the grate isn't directly over the flare for a minute rather than
      panicking about it; there's no damper to choke the fire down with.

      Sugar scorches on open direct heat with no way to back off, which is why variant B
      never puts a dry sugared rub anywhere near the drum — by the time that variant reaches
      the fire the sugar is already dissolved into a wet sauce, which behaves completely
      differently under direct heat (it lacquers instead of burning black).
  - name: variant A — sliced (texas style)
    ingredients:
      - item: cooking salt
        note: 1% of brisket weight — kosher; e.g. ~20g for a 2kg piece. reduce 25% for table salt, increase 50% for flakes
      - item: coarse black pepper
        note: matched to the salt by volume, which is roughly 60-80% of it by weight (pepper is less dense) — e.g. ~14g alongside 20g salt
      - item: liquid smoke dilution
        optional: true
        note: dose by surface area not weight. pork-ribs runs ~5.4g/kg into a spray of water for a flat rack shape, and a brisket flat has similar surface-per-mass — as a concrete example, a 2kg flat is roughly 10-11g liquid smoke into ~120g water. arrive under and add drops; it turns acrid fast with no way back
    steps:
      - dry brine — rub salt then pepper over every face, uncovered on a rack in the fridge overnight (4 hrs minimum)
      - out of the fridge 1 hr before cooking
      - build the drum's fire well ahead so it's settled to coals by the time you need it
      - drum pass (optional but recommended) — straight onto the hot grate over the coal bed, 15-20 mins, turning once. this is the smoke-pickup and bark-kickstart window, while the surface is still damp and takes smoke best — see "the drum". skip it entirely and go straight to the oven if the fire isn't going; you lose the smoke kiss but nothing else changes
      - into the oven, 115-120°C fan-forced, wire rack over a dry drip tray for max bark or a wet one as insurance against drying out
      - if using the liquid smoke dilution, first mop once the surface looks dry rather than wet or beaded — usually 45 mins-1 hr in — then again every 45 mins-1 hr while it's still unwrapped, 2-3 passes total before you wrap. stop mopping the moment it's wrapped; see notes on why
      - uncovered until core hits 70-75°C and the bark is dark and set — roughly 2.5-3.5 hrs depending on thickness, go by the look of the bark and the temp, not the clock
      - wrap tight — foil for a thin flat, butcher paper for a fattier point — with a splash of the mop inside
      - back in until core reaches 96-99°C and a skewer meets no resistance anywhere, checking several spots
      - rest wrapped at least 1 hr before anything else — the rest works while the temperature is falling, so don't go from the wrap straight into a hold
      - optional second drum pass — unwrap, straight back onto the hot grate 3-5 mins a side to re-firm the bark the wrap softened. watch it continuously, it goes from perfect to burnt in under a minute with no lid to choke the heat back
      - slice against the grain, pencil-thick
    notes: |
      If it's ready early: rest wrapped on the bench 1 hr first (never go from 90°C+ straight
      into a warm hold — the rest only works while the temperature is actively falling), then
      wrapped-roast-plus-towels into a pre-warmed esky for up to ~3 hrs, which sits it around
      60-70°C, the gentlest option. Longer than that, move to a 65°C oven instead, topping up
      the wrap liquid. 60°C is the safety line — above it you're fine indefinitely, below it
      you're in the danger zone, and an esky drifts down over time so don't just hope it holds.
      A cut this size has noticeably less thermal mass than a full pork shoulder and drifts
      under 60°C faster, so lean toward the oven sooner than you would for a big roast.

      Mopping stops at the wrap because from there the meat is sealed and sitting in its own
      steam for hours — more liquid smoke added to that just concentrates and turns harsh,
      the same reason the wrap liquid itself is kept plain rather than more mop. Everything
      the mop is going to contribute happens in the open, unwrapped phase, which is also why
      it's timed to a schedule instead of a single dousing: a single early mop dries out and
      stops contributing well before the bark is actually set.
  - name: variant B — rub
    ingredients:
      - item: brown sugar
        mass: 20
        qty: 1
        unit: tbsp
        note: sub white. per 1kg brisket — scale linearly to your weight
      - item: paprika
        mass: 5
        qty: 2
        unit: tsp
      - item: garlic powder
        mass: 2.5
        qty: 1
        unit: tsp
        note: sub 1 tsp fresh garlic
      - item: onion powder
        mass: 1.5
        qty: 1
        unit: tsp
        note: sub garlic powder
      - item: cumin powder
        qty: 0.5
        unit: tsp
      - item: mustard powder
        mass: 2.5
        qty: 0.75
        unit: tsp
      - item: cooking salt
        qty: 1
        unit: tsp
        note: kosher — reduce 25% for table salt, increase 50% for flakes
      - item: black pepper
        mass: 0.5
        qty: 0.5
        unit: tsp
  - name: variant B — sauce
    ingredients:
      - item: garlic
        qty: 2
        unit: clove
        note: minced; sub 2 tsp powder
      - item: apple cider vinegar
        mass: 125
        qty: 125
        unit: ml
      - item: ketchup
        mass: 335
        note: or Aussie tomato sauce
      - item: brown sugar
        mass: 80
        note: loosely packed, not tightly; sub white
      - item: black pepper
        qty: 2
        unit: tsp
      - item: onion powder
        qty: 2
        unit: tsp
      - item: mustard powder
        qty: 2
        unit: tsp
      - item: cayenne pepper
        qty: 1
        unit: tsp
        note: adjust to taste
      - item: worcestershire sauce
        qty: 1
        unit: tbsp
        note: sub soy sauce
      - item: water
        mass: 500
        qty: 500
        unit: ml
        note: only when using this as braising liquid — leave out if reserving the sauce for a finishing toss or glaze instead
    steps:
      - make the sauce (everything except the water) in the baking pan, then stir in the water for the braise. leave the water out entirely if you're making this to toss through variant C instead
  - name: variant B — procedure (braised, oven start to finish)
    steps:
      - oven to 160°C
      - coat brisket in the rub, then in the sauce, meat side down
      - cover with lid or foil, bake 3.5 hrs or until it pulls apart under a fork, basting intermittently
      - lift the meat out, spoon more sauce over, and strain the braising liquid
      - drum finish (optional) — only worth doing if you've already got the fire down to a coal bed; skip it without any loss to the dish, a hot oven grill does the same job. reduce the strained sauce a little on the stove first if it's thin. lay the meat directly on the hot grate over the coals (never over open flame — that's what scorches the sugar), baste with the reduced sauce every 2-3 mins, turning once, for about 8-12 mins total, until the sauce has tackified into a lacquer and the edges have caught a little char
      - oven alternative to the drum finish — same basting cadence, under a hot grill/broiler on a foil-lined tray. broilers run hotter and closer than a coal bed, so watch it more closely and expect it to be done sooner
    notes: |
      Most forgiving of the three by a wide margin, and it's the safe fallback for a genuinely
      poor cheap cut — you lose the bark and it stops reading as "brisket" specifically, but
      that's the right trade for a bad piece rather than a failure.

      This variant never has an unwrapped, undressed phase — it's coated and covered from the
      very first minute in the oven — so there's no window for it to pick up real smoke the
      way A and C can on the drum's opening pass. A few drops of liquid smoke stirred into the
      sauce's water component is worth considering here if you want any smoke character at all
      out of this one.
  - name: variant C — chopped or pulled (lean/thin flat)
    ingredients:
      - item: cooking salt
        note: 1% of brisket weight for the dry brine, same as variant A — or see the wet brine alternative below
      - item: coarse black pepper
        note: matched to the salt by volume, as variant A — dose off the pre-brine weight, since a wet-brined piece goes in a few percent heavier
      - item: salt
        optional: true
        note: |
          for a wet brine, instead of — never as well as — the dry brine above. either 5% in water for
          12-24 hrs, or by equilibrium at 1-1.25% of the combined meat-plus-water weight, which cannot
          over-salt no matter how long it sits. a rescue lever for a genuinely poor lean flat, not a
          general upgrade; see notes
      - item: liquid smoke dilution
        optional: true
        note: same dilution as variant A — dosed by surface area, not the wet brine's weight. use the same ~5g/kg-into-water ratio and arrive under
      - item: salted stock or apple juice
        note: lightly salted (~1%) only — this goes in the foil at the wrap, not the liquid-smoke mop. use it plain and unsalted if you wet brined, the meat is already carrying that salt
      - item: butter or reserved rendered fat
        note: a couple of tablespoons, melted through at the very end, tasting as you add it
    steps:
      - dry brine overnight as variant A, or wet brine instead if you have the time — a lean flat holds moisture better coming out of a wet brine than a dry one. brine chilled and fully submerged, weighing the flat down so it doesn't float
      - if wet brined — pat dry hard, then uncovered on a rack in the fridge 4-12 hrs to dry the surface back before anything else. skipping this costs you the bark and most of the smoke; it also means a wet brine wants more lead time than a dry one, not less
      - drum pass exactly as variant A (optional) — 15-20 mins over the coal bed, turning once, while the surface is still damp
      - oven to 115-120°C fan-forced, water in the drip tray this time — lean cut, take the humidity over the bark, since there's much less fat here to protect it from drying out
      - if using the liquid smoke dilution, mop on the same schedule as variant A — once the surface looks dry, then every 45 mins-1 hr while unwrapped. this variant's unwrapped window is shorter, since it wraps at 65°C rather than 70-75°C, so expect only 1-2 passes rather than A's 2-3 before you get to the wrap
      - wrap earlier than variant A, at 65°C rather than 70-75°C — a lean cut can't spare the extra time unwrapped that the point-end version can
      - into the foil — a splash of the lightly salted stock or apple juice, not more liquid smoke. sealed in foil for hours, the mop's vinegar keeps working on the meat instead of mostly evaporating like it does on an open surface, and it goes mushy and over-tangy; salt is what actually helps a lean cut hold onto moisture, acid doesn't
      - back in until fully tender and a skewer meets no resistance anywhere — expect this closer to 90-93°C than the 96-99°C of a fattier piece, and go by probe feel over the number
      - rest wrapped 1 hr
      - chop or pull rather than slice
      - toss with the reclaimed wrap juices first — taste before adding any salt, especially if you wet brined; expect more juice than usual out of a wet-brined piece, so go easy on the splash at the wrap
      - melt the butter or reserved fat through next — fat doesn't penetrate meat, it coats the outside of already-separated strands, so it only does anything once the meat is chopped and the surface area is enormous. doing this before chopping is wasted
      - optional: toss with variant B's sauce to taste at this point, at full strength (leave out that sauce's 500g water — that was there to make a braising liquid, not a finishing sauce) — see notes. that sauce is salty in its own right, so taste as it goes in rather than tipping the lot
    notes: |
      This is the option that actually rescues a cheap thin flat — slicing exposes every dry
      fibre in a lean piece, chopping and tossing in juice and fat hides exactly that. No
      second drum pass needed here the way variant A has one; the chopped surface hides what
      slicing would otherwise expose, so there's nothing left to re-firm.

      For "bbq pulled meat" in the usual sense, this is the variant to land on rather than
      variant B. B is sauced and covered from the first minute and never builds bark or takes
      on smoke, so what falls apart there is closer to a bbq-sauce pot roast than to pulled
      brisket — it just happens to also be shreddable. This variant builds real bark, can pick
      up real smoke on the drum's opening pass, gets taken to genuinely tender, and only then
      gets tossed — which is the actual sequence and texture of pulled brisket. The only piece
      it's missing versus a classic sauced pulled brisket is the sauce itself, which is why
      variant B's sauce is offered here as a final toss rather than a braising liquid: make it,
      skip the water, and stir it through to taste after the juices and fat rather than before
      — that way you're seasoning the outside of already-separated meat instead of stewing in
      it, which is the whole point of pulling before saucing.

      On whether the wet brine is worth it: the gain is real but modest, and narrower than it's
      usually sold as. Salt lets the muscle hold more water and shrink less, and it goes in carrying
      5-8% extra weight — but brisket is the worst case for that, since hours held at 90°C+ drives
      most of the added water back out again. What saves it here specifically is that this variant
      reclaims the purge and tosses it back through the chopped meat, so the water takes a detour
      through the foil and comes back as juice rather than being lost. The other genuine benefit is
      insurance: a wet-brined lean flat is more forgiving of being pushed too far. Against that you
      pay in bark, a slightly bouncy ham-like texture, an extra day of lead time, and several ways to
      double up on salt. Worth it for a poor, thin, lean flat where dryness is the actual risk being
      managed; for anything decent, dry brine and don't bother.

      Salt stacks in four places once you wet brine, and all four need backing off: no salt in the
      rub that follows, plain rather than salted liquid in the foil, taste the reclaimed juices before
      correcting them, and taste variant B's sauce in rather than tipping it in. The general note about
      checking the piece isn't already wet-cured for corning matters double here — pre-cured plus a
      brine is inedible, with nothing to be done about it.

      On brine strength: 5% for the full 24 hrs is the aggressive end, and a thin flat under ~4cm
      equilibrates fast enough to come out tasting like salt beef. Use 12 hrs at that strength for a
      thin piece and save 24 for something 5cm or thicker — or sidestep the clock entirely with the
      equilibrium option, which is the better fit for a cut whose thickness you're guessing at anyway.

      Same holding logic as variant A applies if it's ready early: rest 1 hr, esky up to ~3
      hrs, 65°C oven beyond that, 60°C is the line. Once it's shredded, though, hold it
      differently to a whole roast — shredded meat has huge surface area and dries and cools
      fast, so don't shred until just before serving, and if it has to wait, keep it whole and
      wrapped and shred at the last minute.
notes: |
  Match the variant to the cut, not the other way round. Point end (fatty, 20-30% fat)
  behaves like pork shoulder and is forgiving of any of the three. Flat or navel (lean,
  8-12%) wants variant C's earlier wrap and pulled treatment. A thin cheap flat under ~4cm —
  which is what cheap brisket usually is — defaults to C unless you're confident in the piece.

  Check it isn't already wet-cured for corning before doing anything else with it. It'll be
  pink and noticeably salty straight out of the bag, and every salt number above assumes it
  isn't already seasoned.

  No curing salt anywhere here, unlike pork-ribs — that's there for a pink smoke ring nobody
  needs to see through a knife cut on a piece this size, and the drum pass gives a genuine if
  faint one anyway, from real wood smoke.

  If separating a whole packer into point and flat, do it before cooking whichever variant —
  their grains run roughly 90° apart, and no single slicing or chopping angle is right for
  both once they've become one cooked mass.
